# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Glossary batch 7 — nuclear physics, diagnostics hardware, materials engineering."""
def register(cat, page, F):
    T = [
     ("binding-energy","Nuclear Binding Energy","The energy holding a nucleus together — and the source of fusion's power.","Binding energy is what holds protons and neutrons together; the binding-energy-per-nucleon curve peaks near iron. Fusing light nuclei climbs toward that peak, releasing the difference as energy — the origin of fusion power.",["mass-defect","fusion-basics","the-dt-reaction"]),
     ("mass-defect","Mass Defect","The tiny mass converted to energy in every fusion reaction.","A fused nucleus weighs slightly less than its parts; that missing mass becomes energy via E = mc&sup2;. The mass defect is small per reaction but, multiplied by Avogadro's number, makes fusion millions of times more energy-dense than chemistry.",["binding-energy","fusion-basics","mev-unit"]),
     ("neutron-spectrum","Neutron Energy Spectrum","The distribution of neutron energies a machine produces — it sets damage and breeding.","D–T fusion produces a sharp 14 MeV neutron line; scattering in the blanket softens the spectrum. The spectrum shapes both material damage and tritium breeding, and is a key input to neutronics design.",["neutron-flux","neutron-moderation","dpa"]),
     ("neutron-capture","Neutron Capture","A nucleus absorbing a neutron — the reaction that breeds tritium and activates materials.","When a nucleus absorbs a neutron it can breed a useful isotope (tritium from lithium) or become radioactive (activation). Managing which captures happen where is the essence of blanket and shield design.",["tritium-breeding","activation-products","neutron-multiplier"]),
     ("transmutation","Transmutation","Neutron reactions changing one element into another — useful for breeding, unwanted in structure.","Neutrons transmute nuclei into different elements: deliberately, to breed tritium; incidentally, to activate structural steel. Low-activation materials are chosen so incidental transmutation yields only short-lived isotopes.",["neutron-capture","activation-products","low-activation-steel"]),
     ("beta-decay","Beta Decay","The radioactive decay by which tritium and activation products relax.","Beta decay emits an electron as a neutron converts to a proton; tritium decays this way with a ~12-year half-life, as do many activation products. Its low-energy electrons are easily shielded but require containment for tritium.",["tritium","activation-products","tritium-safety"]),
     ("cyclotron-cutoff","Wave Cutoffs and Resonances","Density and field thresholds that decide which heating waves reach the plasma core.","A plasma reflects waves below a cutoff density and absorbs them at resonances. Heating-system design threads waves past cutoffs to their resonant layer, which is why launch frequency and geometry matter so much.",["electron-cyclotron-heating","plasma-frequency","mode-conversion"]),
     ("mode-conversion","Mode Conversion","One plasma wave transforming into another, exploited for heating.","Where wave conditions change, one wave mode can convert into another that deposits energy more effectively. Mode-conversion schemes are used to heat ions efficiently with RF power.",["ion-cyclotron-heating","cyclotron-cutoff","wave-heating"]),
     ("wave-heating","Wave (RF) Heating","Depositing power by launching electromagnetic waves that the plasma absorbs.","RF heating launches waves tuned to plasma resonances — ion-cyclotron, lower-hybrid, electron-cyclotron — that the plasma absorbs. It is precise and steerable, complementing neutral beams.",["ion-cyclotron-heating","electron-cyclotron-heating","heating-systems"]),
     ("ece-diagnostic","Electron-Cyclotron Emission Diagnostic","Reads the plasma's own microwave glow to map electron temperature.","The plasma emits at the electron-cyclotron frequency, and because that frequency maps to position via the field, the emission gives a radial electron-temperature profile continuously — a core diagnostic.",["thomson-scattering","electron-temperature","plasma-diagnostics"]),
     ("reflectometry","Reflectometry","Bouncing microwaves off density layers to profile the plasma.","Reflectometry launches microwaves that reflect at specific density layers; timing the echoes builds a density profile and senses fluctuations. It is compact and needs little port access.",["interferometry","plasma-density","plasma-diagnostics"]),
     ("neutron-camera","Neutron Camera","Images where fusion is happening by mapping neutron emission.","A neutron camera views the plasma through collimated sightlines to map where neutrons — and thus fusion reactions — originate, giving a direct picture of the fusion power distribution.",["neutron-diagnostics","the-neutron-economy","plasma-diagnostics"]),
     ("rogowski-coil","Rogowski Coil","A loop that measures total plasma current.","A Rogowski coil is a toroidal winding around the plasma whose signal is proportional to the enclosed current, giving a direct, robust measurement of the total plasma current.",["plasma-current","magnetic-diagnostics","diamagnetic-loop"]),
     ("diamagnetic-loop","Diamagnetic Loop","A sensor that measures the plasma's stored energy.","A diamagnetic loop senses the change in toroidal flux caused by plasma pressure, yielding the plasma's stored thermal energy — a key input to confinement and disruption assessment.",["stored-plasma-energy","magnetic-diagnostics","plasma-beta"]),
     ("thermal-cycling","Thermal Cycling","Repeated heating and cooling that fatigues components.","Every start-up and shutdown thermally cycles components, driving fatigue in plasma-facing parts, welds and magnets. Designing for many cycles is part of ensuring plant lifetime.",["magnet-fatigue","first-wall","plant-availability"]),
     ("radiation-swelling","Radiation Swelling","Neutron-induced volume growth in structural materials.","Neutron damage can cause materials to swell as internal voids form, distorting components over their life. It is one of the degradation mechanisms low-activation structural steels are selected to resist.",["neutron-damage-materials","dpa","radiation-embrittlement"]),
     ("radiation-embrittlement","Radiation Embrittlement","Loss of ductility in materials under neutron irradiation.","Neutron irradiation can make structural metals more brittle, reducing their tolerance to stress. Managing embrittlement over the component lifetime is central to materials selection and is heaviest on the breeder.",["neutron-damage-materials","radiation-swelling","low-activation-steel"]),
     ("hip-bonding","Hot Isostatic Pressing","A bonding process that joins plasma-facing armour to heat sinks.","Hot isostatic pressing bonds materials under high temperature and pressure, used to join tungsten armour to copper-alloy heat sinks in first-wall and divertor components. It produces strong, void-free joints for high heat flux.",["plasma-facing-materials","cucrzr","tungsten-divertor"]),
     ("electron-beam-welding","Electron-Beam Welding","A precise, deep welding method for vessel sectors.","Electron-beam and other automated welding methods join thick vessel sectors with deep, narrow welds and low distortion, meeting the dimensional and integrity requirements of the vacuum boundary.",["vacuum-vessel-sector","non-destructive-examination","systems-integration"]),
     ("non-destructive-examination","Non-Destructive Examination (NDE)","Inspecting welds and components without damaging them.","NDE — ultrasonic, radiographic, dye-penetrant methods — verifies weld and component integrity without destroying the part. It is essential to qualifying the vessel and structural welds to code.",["electron-beam-welding","materials-qualification","vacuum-vessel"]),
     ("tolerance-stackup","Tolerance Stack-Up","How individual part tolerances accumulate across an assembly.","In a precise machine, each part's tolerance adds up across an assembly; tolerance stack-up analysis ensures the whole still fits and functions. It is fundamental to integrating high-precision magnets and vessels.",["systems-integration","non-destructive-examination","general-arrangement"]),
     ("grid-interconnection","Grid Interconnection","Connecting a plant's output to the electrical grid.","A power plant must interconnect to the grid with equipment that matches voltage, frequency and protection requirements. For fusion this is conventional high-voltage engineering, part of the balance of plant.",["balance-of-plant","power-quality","grid-scale-power"]),
     ("power-quality","Power Quality","Delivering clean, stable electricity to the grid.","Power quality covers voltage stability, frequency and harmonics of the delivered electricity. A fusion plant's conversion and grid-interface systems must meet grid codes like any generator.",["grid-interconnection","power-conversion","dispatchable-power"]),
     ("combined-heat-power","Combined Heat and Power","Using a plant's waste heat as well as its electricity.","Capturing a plant's waste heat for industrial or district use raises overall energy efficiency. A fusion plant's firm high-grade heat suits combined heat-and-power alongside electricity.",["industrial-heat-hydrogen","power-conversion","baseload-power"]),
     ("islanding","Islanding & Black Start","Running independently of the grid — valuable for resilience.","Islanding lets a plant power a local load independent of the wider grid, and black-start capability lets it restart the grid after an outage. These resilience features are especially valuable for defence and critical-infrastructure siting.",["defense-power","microgrid-fusion","grid-interconnection"]),
     ("microgrid-fusion","Fusion in a Microgrid","Compact fusion as the firm anchor of a local microgrid.","A compact fusion generator can anchor a microgrid — a self-contained local grid — providing firm power that renewables and storage balance around. It suits resilient, independent installations.",["islanding","defense-power","baseload-power"]),
     ("neutron-activation-analysis","Neutron Activation Analysis","Using neutrons to identify materials — a breeder-enabled capability.","Bombarding a sample with neutrons and reading the induced radioactivity identifies its elements with great sensitivity. A high-flux neutron source enables this analytical technique as part of the neutron economy.",["the-neutron-economy","medical-isotopes","neutron-flux"]),
     ("specific-power","Specific Power","Power produced per unit mass or volume — a compactness metric.","Specific power measures how much power a machine makes for its size or mass; high specific power means a compact, material-efficient plant. High field and high beta are the levers that raise it.",["plasma-pressure","high-field-magnets","compact-vs-conventional"]),
     ("availability-growth","Availability Growth","How plant uptime is expected to improve with operating experience.","New plant types improve their availability as operators learn and components mature — an 'availability growth' curve. Kronos treats early availability as an open study, expecting improvement rather than assuming a mature value at start.",["plant-availability","capacity-factor","the-path-to-build"]),
     ("commissioning","Commissioning","Bringing a built machine to life, system by system, up to first plasma.","Commissioning tests each system cold, then integrates them — energising magnets, pumping down, checking interlocks — culminating in first plasma. It is the bridge between construction and operation on the path to build.",["first-plasma","the-path-to-build","tokamak-startup"]),
     ("acceptance-testing","Acceptance Testing","Proving each component meets spec before it goes into the machine.","Before installation, major components are acceptance-tested — magnets powered to field, vessels pressure- and leak-tested — against their specifications. It is where materials and manufacturing qualification meet the real hardware.",["materials-qualification","commissioning","non-destructive-examination"]),
     ("design-basis","Design Basis","The set of conditions a machine is engineered to withstand.","The design basis defines the loads, temperatures, fields and fault conditions a component must handle. Kronos states results 'within the design basis' — e.g. self-protecting magnets — to be precise about what has and has not been shown.",["quench-protection-detail","gate-reviews","the-path-to-build"]),
     ("factor-of-safety","Factor of Safety","The margin between design loads and failure.","A factor of safety is the ratio of a component's capacity to the load it actually sees. Adequate margins on stress, fatigue and thermal limits are what turn a design point into a buildable machine.",["hoop-stress","magnet-fatigue","design-basis"]),
     ("redundancy","Redundancy & Fault Tolerance","Duplicated systems that keep a machine safe when something fails.","Safety-critical functions are duplicated so a single failure cannot cause harm — redundant sensors, independent trip paths, backup power. It is a core principle of the safety-instrumented system.",["sis-controller","inherent-safety","instrumentation-control"]),
     ("maintainability","Maintainability","Designing a machine so it can actually be maintained.","Because an operated machine is activated, maintainability — remote-handling access, modular components, replaceable panels — is designed in from the start. It is a first-order driver of plant availability.",["remote-handling","plant-availability","blanket-module-component"]),
    ]
    for slug,term,one,para,rel in T:
        page(slug,term,"glossary",one,one,[('p',para)],related=rel,jtype="DefinedTerm",kicker="Glossary")
